/-!
# Error codes

The crate is adapted from the presentation "Rust for Linux: Code Documentation &
Tests" of the Linux Foundation mentorship series. The type `ErrorCode` wraps a
Linux kernel error code. The kernel returns errors as negative numbers, such as
`-EPERM` or `-ENOMEM`; the type `ErrorCode` stores the positive number, from `1`
to `MAX_ERROR_CODE = 4095`. The bound `4095` is the kernel's constant
`MAX_ERRNO`, which lets a function return either a valid pointer or an error
code in the same value:

```rust
pub const MAX_ERROR_CODE: i32 = 4095;

pub struct ErrorCode {
    value: i32,
}

impl ErrorCode {
    pub fn from_code(code: i32) -> Result<Self, ()> {
        if code <= 0 || code > MAX_ERROR_CODE {
            return Err(());
        }
        Ok(ErrorCode { value: code })
    }

    pub fn to_code(self) -> i32 {
        self.value
    }
}
```

The field `value` is private: it is visible only in the module that declares
it and in the modules inside it. Here that module is the root of the crate, so
code in other crates can construct an `ErrorCode` only through `from_code`,
which accepts only codes between `1` and `MAX_ERROR_CODE`. Every `ErrorCode`
constructed in other crates therefore satisfies `0 < value ≤ MAX_ERROR_CODE`.
A property that every way of constructing a value establishes and every
operation preserves, so that every value satisfies it, is an **invariant**. The
Lean `structure` below does not record this invariant, so it has to be stated
and proved.

